content.text:
Remove unused _q3? fine to leave; lint not enforced. Could remove later.

Now tracking.js. Implement MotionTracker with GPU→CPU fallback, rVFC loop, timing, face skipping, errors.

Important: MediaPipe `PoseLandmarker.createFromOptions` with delegate 'GPU' may throw "Failed to create..." in headless. We only load when camera granted, so fine.

`FilesetResolver.forVisionTasks(wasmPath)` uses `import.meta.url`? passing local path. If app served from subpath (omgithub preview), relative './vendor' works.

Need robust fallback: try GPU, catch, then CPU. If GPU pose works but face GPU fails, use CPU for face.

Timestamps: detection calls with same `now` for both landmarkers okay. But if we skip face frames, still timestamps increase.

Loop via video.requestVideoFrameCallback (rVFC) - callback gets (now, metadata). Use performance.now for detect. Need request next callback; cancel on stop.

Some browsers lack rVFC (Safari 15.4+ has it; older fallback rAF).

Adaptive faceRate 1..3.

Error handling: catch detection errors; if >3 consecutive, call onError and stop.

The result objects: `poseResult.worldLandmarks`, `poseResult.landmarks`; `faceResult.faceLandmarks`, `faceResult.faceBlendshapes`.

onPose({world, normalized}) maybe simpler pass full result.

Implement:

```js
export class MotionTracker {
  constructor({video, onPose, onFace, onMeta, onError}) {...}
  async init(onPhase) {
    const vision = await FilesetResolver.forVisionTasks(WASM);
    onPhase?.('pose');
    this.pose = await createWithFallback(...);
    this.delegate = ...
    if (this.faceEnabled) this.face = await ...
  }
  ...
}
```

createWithFallback:
```js
async function createLandmarker(kind, vision, opts) {
  try { return await Ctor.createFromOptions(vision, {...opts, baseOptions:{...,delegate:'GPU'}}); }
  catch (e) { if (delegate forced?) throw? }
}
```
Better return {landmarker, delegate}. Pose must succeed; face optional. On face failure, set face=null and report face unavailable (but still pose). Set faceEnabled=false.

Models: FaceLandmarker.createFromOptions with outputFaceBlendshapes true.

Loop:
```js
_startLoop() {
  const video = this.video;
  const step = () => {
    if (!this.running) return;
    if (video.readyState >= 2 && video.videoWidth) this._process();
    this._schedule();
  };
  this._schedule = () => {
    if (!this.running) return;
    if (video.requestVideoFrameCallback) this._rvfc = video.requestVideoFrameCallback(step);
    else this._raf = requestAnimationFrame(step);
  };
  this._schedule();
}
```
Careful `step` must be stable reference. Fine.

_process:
```js
const now = performance.now();
let t0 = now;
if (this._faceSkipCounter++ % this.faceEvery === 0) face detect after pose? both same timestamp.
const pose = this.pose.detectForVideo(video, now);
if (face && enabled && frame % n === 0) faceRes = this.face.detectForVideo(video, now);
inference = performance.now() - t0
onPose(pose); onFace(faceRes or null) 
onMeta({ms, delegate, fps...})
```
Calling both with same timestamp not an issue (separate graphs).

If face detect only on some frames, still call onFace(null) on skipped frames? No—hasFace decays after 420ms; 30/3=10fps fine. But if face disabled, no calls → hasFace false.

`setFaceEnabled(enabled)`: if disabled and face exists just skip; when re-enabled skip false.

`setFaceEvery(n)`.

`stop()`: running false, cancel rVFC/rAF.
`close()`: landmarkers close.

`init` progress phases via onPhase('wasm'|'pose'|'face'|'ready').

Time between init model loads could be long; UI text updates.

Note if face model load fails, we shouldn't fail overall. catch and report.

Let's write.

