0 {"mode":"live","masked":true,"poseActive":false,"fps":2.903038513644287,"quality":"BALANCED","context":"WebGL 2.0 (OpenGL ES 3.0 Chromium)","tracker":{"pose":true,"face":true,"delegate":"GPU","faceDelegate":"GPU","running":true,"faceEnabled":true},"video":{"w":640,"h":480,"ready":4},"status":"Tracking live","hint":"Step back so your whole body is in frame","landmark":"33 POSE · 478 FACE · GPU","latency":"27651"}
ERRORS [
  "console: INFO: Created TensorFlow Lite XNNPACK delegate for CPU."
]

Live pipeline works: pose and face loaded GPU, camera 640x480, running. Latency 27,651 ms?! That's huge — inference per frame 27 seconds? But FPS 2.9. Something is wrong: `handleMeta` latency shows 27651. This is likely because the first detect call compiled shaders and took 27s; subsequent frames should be normal. Our script only printed one second. But 27.6s for GPU inference on SwiftShader (software GPU) is plausible. On real hardware it will be fast. Still, latency display "27651 MS" ugly. We should clamp/format: show `27.6s` or `>999`. Also MediaPipe GPU on SwiftShader may actually run slowly; real GPU fast. Fine.

Also note the app renders 2.9 fps with heavy inference; in CI screenshot demo mode avoids this.

But `status live`, models loaded, camera works. Face tracking enabled. Good.

Potential concern: The demo mode automation forced by `navigator.webdriver` could apply to users opening in an embedded webview that sets webdriver? Rare.

Now, real inference CPU/GPU in browser: pose lite + face at 640x480; GPU delegate should be ~10-20ms total on a typical laptop. Good.

Performance issue: running both pose and face every frame (30fps) plus 3D. Adaptive doesn't know vision latency; we could adapt faceEvery based on inference ms too. Add: if inference > 35ms, set faceEvery 2; >60 → 3; if <25 → 1. This is smart. Implement in handleMeta with cooldown to avoid oscillation:
```js
state.visionMs = meta.inference smoothed
if now - lastAdapt > 2000:
  if (ema > 50) tracker.setFaceEvery(3)
  else if (ema > 30) 2
  else 1
```
But `inference` includes both models; faceEvery affects it. Add smoothing. Also maybe skip pose every other frame on low-end, but pose is essential.

Also UI latency should show nice value. Format if >1000 → seconds with 1 decimal.

More important: the actual app's live detection with both models at 30fps might be too heavy on mid phones. Face every 2 frames is fine (15fps face). For "Optimize performance", set default faceEvery 1 but adaptive by inference. Also pose lite model.

Use `minFaceEvery`? Fine.

Memory: MediaPipe GPU + Three.js WebGL contexts share GPU; okay.

Another concern: `_process` calls pose.detectForVideo then face.detectForVideo; timestamps identical. Face landmarker may complain timestamp not monotonically increasing? It's per landmarker, so fine. It doesn't throw (tested).

Now, our live test shows the FPS 2.9 due to SwiftShader, but quality adapted to BALANCED. Fine.

Need to clean up live test process/browser (script closed). Check no stray chrome.

Now improve latency formatting and vision adaptation.

Also fix the capture readiness: The hint "Step back..." shows correctly. The camera panel in live would show video with skeleton overlay; good.

Need to test the camera preview overlay with fake camera: it won't detect pose, so no skeleton. Fine.

Now mobile layout. Use playwright-cli resize to 390x844 and screenshot. Need to reload with ?demo=1 to auto start. Use playwright-cli resize. Then screenshot. Let's do after changes.

Also test no-JS errors; console only TensorFlow info (an "error" type due to console.error from MediaPipe? The message is INFO but logged as error by glog). Not a real issue; maybe suppress? Can't.

Now check start screen at 1440x900 and mobile. Screenshot capture default uses 1440x900 and 390x844. Our viewport currently 1050x793 due to playwright-cli default window. Use resize 1440 900 to see desktop as captured.

Let's apply latency & adaptive changes.

In main:
```js
state.visionEma = 0;
let lastVisionAdapt = 0;
function handleMeta(meta) {
  const ms = meta.inference;
  els.latency.textContent = ms > 999 ? `${(ms/1000).toFixed(1)}s` : String(Math.round(ms));
  state.visionEma = state.visionEma ? state.visionEma*0.85 + ms*0.15 : ms;
  const now = performance.now();
  if (state.mode==='live' && now - state.visionAdaptAt > 2500) {
    state.visionAdaptAt = now;
    const tracker = state.tracker;
    if (tracker && tracker.faceEnabled) {
      const every = state.visionEma > 55 ? 3 : state.visionEma > 32 ? 2 : 1;
      if (every !== tracker.faceEvery) tracker.setFaceEvery(every);
    }
  }
  ...
}
```
Careful: the render quality adapt also sets faceEvery, conflicting. Remove faceEvery setting from adaptQuality; leave it to vision. In adaptQuality, just pixel ratio.

Set `state.visionAdaptAt` property and `visionEma`.

Note latency from GPU should be <30ms; then faceEvery 1. On SwiftShader 27s → 3. Good.

Also face coordinate detection every 3 frames at 30fps still 10fps for expressions; acceptable.

Formatting "s".

Also FPS could be shown 60.

Adaptive render quality should consider FPS but the FPS is also capped by vision block; with inference 27s impossible. Not important.

Let's patch.

